  "notes": "USAGE:\n{避|よ}ける (ichidan verb, reading よける) focuses on the physical act of dodging or stepping aside. The same kanji {避|さ}ける (also ichidan, reading さける) means to avoid or keep away from in a broader sense — avoiding people, topics, risks, etc.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {避|さ}ける (さける) — to avoid in general (abstract or physical); broader meaning\n- {避|よ}ける (よける) — specifically to dodge or physically get out of the way; more concrete\n- {躱|かわ}す — to dodge skillfully, often used in martial arts and sports\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- AをBに{避|よ}ける is not used; instead: Aを{避|よ}ける (dodge A)\n- {道|みち}を{避|よ}ける (get out of the way)\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- ボールを{避|よ}ける (dodge a ball)\n- {車|くるま}を{避|よ}ける (get out of the way of a car)\n- {水|みず}たまりを{避|よ}ける (step around a puddle)",
